(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, considered the implementation of the social security system on occupational risk and health care for the public sector as another new success of the Royal Government of Cambodia in helping to improve the lives of public officials, former civil servants and Veterans to avoid risks when encountering health-related or eventual disasters.
He said this on the occasion of presiding over the dissemination of policies on the implementation of the social security system and handing over the membership cards of the National Social Security Fund (NSSF) to a total of leaders, civil servants and contracted officials. 1,538 people at the Ministry of Commerce on the afternoon of November 20, 2018.
On that occasion, Minister Pan Sosak briefed the ceremony on the brief history and main mission of the UNFPA, which was established by sub-decree in 2007 and was officially launched at the end of 2008 to provide social security services. Effectively, transparently and accountably to individuals subject to the provisions of the Labor Law.
On this basis, the Royal Government of Cambodia has entrusted the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training with the task of organizing and launching a social security system for occupational risk and health care for public officials, former civil servants and veterans. In 2017, more to ensure consistency in social protection in both the public and private sectors.
"Social security policies have made a significant contribution to poverty reduction through the distribution of income across borders, which means that high-income people help the low-income people, the healthy people help the rich. "Illness, disability or people at risk, such as the young, help the elderly, which makes people warm in life, maintain stability and harmony in society as a whole." Mr. Pan Sosak emphasized.
Please be informed that in order to promote welfare and social stability in order to ensure security, income and contribute to poverty reduction, the PRC has focused on the following five factors:
1. Respond appropriately to the actual needs of retired civil servants and veterans. Prevent occupational risks and provide health care services to public officials and retirees. Ensure the appropriateness of the obligation to participate and the benefits received. Strengthen the social and financial sustainability of retired civil servants and veterans. Strengthen harmony by helping each other with equity and solidarity.
