UN Genocide Adviser Welcomes Historic Convictions of Former Khmer Rouge Leaders Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan
(Phnom Penh): The historic UN-backed international tribunal on Friday on the genocide of former Khmer Rouge leaders in Cambodia on genocide charges was welcomed by the UN Special Adviser. Expertise in genocide.
In a statement, Adama Dieng, Special Adviser on the Prevention of Genocide, described the UN-backed tribunal conviction in Cambodia as a "good day for justice", adding that "Justice will prevail and impunity for genocide and other heinous crimes will not be recognized."
Nuon Chea, now 92, a former deputy secretary during the brutal Pol Pot regime and 87-year-old former head of state Khieu Samphan, has been charged with massacre of the Cham Muslim and ethnic Vietnamese communities since April. 1975 to January 1979.
The Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) convicted the two men of grave breaches of the 1949 Geneva Conventions and crimes against humanity in the form of murder, genocide. Slavery, deportation, detention, torture, persecution for political, religious and racial and other inhumane acts against the civilian population in Cambodia during that time.
According to the report, this is the first time that a senior official of Pol Pot, the ruling Communist Party of Kampuchea at the time, has been convicted of genocide.
In a separate statement issued by his spokesman, UN Under-Secretary-General António Guterres, the verdict indicated that the perpetrators of the most heinous crimes could be held accountable. Those crimes have been going on for decades.
"The Deputy Secretary-General extends his condolences to the victims of the genocide, crimes against humanity and grave breaches of the 1949 Geneva Conventions," Guterres said in a statement. The commitment and efforts of all officials who have contributed to the important work of the ECCC and called on Member States to continue to support this tribunal.
Dieng also expressed his support and solidarity with the victims, saying that "all the people who suffered from the atrocities committed by the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia during that time Waiting for justice for a long time. "He hopes the decision will provide them with some compensation and relief."
He says this is also a historic verdict to prevent similar crimes from happening in the future: "Although criminal liability is an important tool in providing justice and reparation to victims. "It also plays an important role in preventing and deterring these crimes as well as helping society to reconcile."
"As we see dangerous violations of fundamental rights and international rules and regulations in many places around the world, this decision is a powerful message, both within and outside the world," he said. "Regionally and globally, those who incite or indulge in heinous crimes will soon be held accountable."
Deputy Governor of Ratanakkiri Province: Traffic accidents that often occur caused by people, vehicles and roads
(Ratanakkiri): Mr. Yim Phen, Deputy Governor of Ratanakkiri Province and Chairman of the Ratanakkiri Provincial Road Safety Sub-Committee, confirmed that traffic accidents in Ratanakkiri Province were caused by three main factors that killed the victims. Forty people were injured and 150 people were seriously injured in the first 11 months of 2018.
Mr. Yim Phen made this remark on the occasion of the 13th International Road Traffic Remembrance Festival, which was held at the Samdech Techo Hun Sen Festival at the Department of Culture and Fine Arts: Ratanakkiri province on November 20.
The Deputy Governor called on all relevant departments, units, authorities at all levels, the private sector and people from all walks of life in Ratanakkiri to increase cooperation and cooperation to participate in law enforcement by respecting the road traffic law. Land for everyone, especially not to let children under 6 years old and let animals walk on the national road.
Speaking on the occasion, Mr. Khim Romney, Deputy Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Ratanakkiri Province and Director of the Secretariat of the Provincial Road Safety Sub-Committee also confirmed that road traffic accidents are still a challenge and a long-term problem that can occur. Immediately in case of road users there is negligence.
"The Secretary-General extends his condolences to the victims of the genocide, crimes against humanity and the grave violations of the 1949 Geneva Conventions, which the two defendants were found guilty of," the statement said. Guilt. ”
Dr. Nguon Nhel advises all rowers to take care of the environment and not to throw plastic bags or water bottles out of the trash.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Nguon Nhel, First Vice President of the National Assembly, on November 20, 2018, met with representatives of water players in Kampong Thom province at the OCIC area of Chroy Changvar satellite city, Phnom Penh and Chea Sim Chroy Changvar High School. The participation of Mr. Sok Lou, Governor of Kampong Thom Province and Mr. Chhun Chhon, Chairman of the Provincial Council, as well as all provincial boat steering committees and boat players.
Mr. Nguon Nhel instructed all water players to respect discipline and internal unity well. He added that all water sports players must eat hygienically, contribute to maintaining a good environment, do not throw plastic bags or water bottles outside the bin, defecate outside the toilet, must pay high attention to safety and health. Personal.
