WASHINGTON: Seven out of 10 Americans believe that all immigrants should have the opportunity to enter the United States, according to a survey conducted by the University of Monmouth. America, and they are not a threat. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Tuesday morning, November 20, 2018.
In its report released on Monday, Mon Mao stated that 29 percent of those interviewed said that migrants from Central American countries were a major threat. For the United States, 24 percent said they had little threat and 39 percent said there was no threat to the United States.
In addition, the majority of Americans who support Republicans, or 54 percent, see immigrants from Central America as a major threat, while only 11 percent of Democrats and 28 percent of independents see it as a major threat. The same.
Currently, tens of thousands of immigrants from Central American countries are flocking to the Mexican border to try to enter the United States. But Donald Trump has ordered nearly 5,800 troops to intervene to stem the flow of refugees. 85 hectares of mixed crops in Mondulkiri have been certified by Organic Agriculture Organization
(Mondulkiri): To date, about 85 hectares of organic farmland of indigenous farmers in 10 villages in Mondulkiri province have been certified by CEDAC, which is why farmers Have the energy to grow more organic crops.
He said that due to the improved cultivation process, the implementation of the right techniques and better connection to the market, the vision of the CEDAC team will certify the quality of this organic to 30 indigenous families. In Busra commune, Pech Reada district, while many other farmers insisted that the CEDAC team to certify the quality of this organ.
The organic cultivation of the people in Mondulkiri province has just started a year, but see that the people in a northeastern province are very active in turning to organic farming, combined with the climate, high altitude, suitable for Tropical crops, adequate rainfall, especially because the soil there is well fertilized.
Uncle Nhim Saly, a Pnong resident of Putreng village, Dak Dam commune, Oreang district, confirmed that before, he and other villagers did not grow as many crops as they do today, but after CEDAC came down to train them. After maintaining the promotion as well as finding markets for agricultural products, the people in Putreng village have grown more crops such as coffee, oranges and vines.
Mr. Sali stated that today he has more than 10 hectares of land for coffee cultivation, that is, there is a lot of plantation, there is no shortage, only lack of planting power. However, about 200 coffee trees are already grown on his uncle's farm.
Ms. Tong Chantheang, Director of CEDAC, stated that helping farmers in Mondulkiri to grow organic crops is to turn their habit of rotating crops into organic crops that are worth the market. Instead, it aims to improve their lives.
On the other hand, helping them to know how to do organic farming is to prevent them from continuing to hunt wild animals, cut down forests or engage in any business that leads to the destruction of natural resources in this beautiful northeastern province.
