(Sihanoukville): The Asia Injury Prevention Foundation has partnered with the National Committee for Road Safety and the Sihanoukville Provincial Police in conducting educational activities to promote the wearing of helmets on the road to encourage greater attention. From road users in Sihanoukville.
According to the report, in Cambodia, about 1,976 people died in traffic accidents in 2017, of which children aged 0 to 14 accounted for nearly 150 of these deaths, which is more than the number. Deaths from malaria, dengue fever and landmines combined.
Road fatalities increased by almost 10% in Sihanoukville in 2017, making Sihanoukville the 12th highest accident province.
Kim Panha, director of the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation, said that although in Cambodia in 2017, about 75 percent of traffic fatalities were motorcyclists, only 27 percent were adults and passengers, including children. Only 8% wore a helmet.
