A third-grade student was electrocuted by a blacksmith, and the father of the victim complained to the Russey Keo district police to arrest him.
The Deputy Criminal Inspector of Russey Keo District confirmed that after the father of the victim boy met with this tragic incident, he did not report it to the police, taking his son's body to prepare for the traditional ceremony at Prek Thmey village, Chey Thom commune, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province.
He said that because before the incident on the 13th, that is, six days ago, the victim was found dead, causing a surprise to the local police in cooperation with the Office of Technical and Scientific of the Police Commission. Phnom Penh to inspect and evaluate the conclusion that the suspect is a murder case.
The Deputy Inspector further stated that the victim was named Sithong, male, 11 years old, working at Tuol Sangke School, 3rd grade, living with his father, Ith Sitha, 35 years old, working as a construction worker, living in a house with no number along the dirt road. Group 3, Boeung Chhouk village, Sangkat Kilometer 6, Khan Russey Keo. As for the perpetrator, his name is Nuth Sophanna, male, 31 years old, and he works as a welder.
It should be noted that the suspects involved in the above murder, along with exhibits, have been under temporary detention at the Russey Keo District Police Inspectorate to build a case to proceed with the procedure. Tomorrow morning, outgoing US Ambassador to Cambodia will bid farewell to Samdech Techo Hun Sen at the Peace Palace
Senior Minister Ly Thuch: Samdech Techo Hun Sen Initiates and Supports the Establishment of the ASEAN Regional Center for Mine Action
(Phnom Penh): On the afternoon of November 19, 2018, the Mine Authority held a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) signing ceremony between the Cambodian Authority for Mine Action and Mine Action and the ASEAN Regional Center for Mine Action.
Mr. Prum Sopheak Mongkul, Secretary General of the Mine Authority and Mr. Ly Panharith, Director General of the ASEAN Regional Center for Mine Action, signed the Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) from Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Deputy Chairman of the Mine Authority, Representative of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Cooperation. International RCAF / NPMEC and CMAC.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch stated that through this Memorandum, the two sides will continue to seek support for the implementation of mine action in Cambodia and at the regional level, as well as the exchange of experiences between ASEAN member countries, as well as the establishment of an agreement on Partnerships between ASEAN Member States and donors or the international community.
At the same time, the Senior Minister would like to express his deep gratitude to Samdech Techo Prime Minister for initiating and supporting the establishment of the ASEAN Regional Center for Mine Action so that all countries in the ASEAN region have the opportunity to share experiences and help each other. Mutual assistance in dealing with landmines and explosive remnants of war in all ASEAN countries.
In the next 3-5 years, CEDAC will supply enough local organic coffee. Currently, Cambodia needs about 10,000 tons of coffee per year, mostly imported from Vietnam, Laos and Thailand.
(Mondulkiri): As Cambodians now consume more and more coffee, and because domestic coffee is still imported, the Cambodian Center for Agricultural Studies and Development (CEDAC) has revealed its market supply direction. Organic coffee in the country enough to meet demand in the next 3-5 years and also destined for export.
During a visit to Mondulkiri organic coffee plantation last weekend, CEDAC representatives estimated that Cambodia currently needs about 10,000 tonnes of coffee a year, most of which is imported. From Vietnam, Laos, Thailand and so on.
However, CEDAC said that the import of coffee has not significantly affected the local market because local coffee is not yet sufficient, but these are opportunities for local farmers and producers. Intended and is doing this business instead.
CEDAC expects that in the next 3-5 years, Cambodia will be able to produce more organic coffee on its own and be able to supply enough to the local market, reducing coffee imports even more. For a year now.
In fact, CEDAC has been working to train indigenous farmers in Mondulkiri to grow and assist with materials and other means, from land preparation, planting and maintenance of coffee, to harvesting. And drying coffee beans, peeling, roasting and packing coffee beans to bring coffee products to special markets are trying to integrate Cambodian organic coffee to compete in overseas markets.
In addition, CEDAC has been working hard to make people more aware of organic coffee, to support and consume locally grown and produced organic coffee with clear sources, inspections and certification from professional and accurate institutions. .
Currently, organic coffee in Mondulkiri is gradually sold in various markets in Phnom Penh, such as CEDAC Organic Market (Okra Market), F Cafe, Robusta Busra, Bingo Coffee, which are all available. Strong support from coffee lovers.
CEDAC's appeal is that consuming organic coffee with a clear source is more beneficial to health, although the price is a bit higher than imported coffee without a clear source, in particular, it is a huge contribution to improving the livelihood of farmers. On the other hand, consuming fake coffee with no clear source or poor quality coffee is not good for the health of the consumer.
