(Phnom Penh): About 800 Cambodians and Japanese people participated in the program "Phnom Penh, a clean city with Japan" walk to collect garbage and clean the courtyards in Phnom Penh this morning, November 18, 2018.
The event was also attended by Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, Mr. Horino Uchihi Hidehisa, Ambassador of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia, and Mr. Huot Hay, Deputy Governor of Phnom Penh, which was co-organized by the Committee Phnom Penh Clean City Project with Japan and the Ministry of Environment.
Japanese Ambassador Horinouchi Hidehisa said that the program was very meaningful as the Japanese people in Phnom Penh initiated the project in collaboration with the Ministry of Environment and Phnom Penh Capital Hall, and the people of Japan and Cambodia united to clean up the city. . The ambassador stressed that the problem of garbage in the city is not only in Cambodia, but also in Japan.
Mr. Huot Hay said that it is necessary for everyone to pay attention to make our city a place to live and hygienic environment.
According to the current report of the Ministry of Environment, in a day, solid waste in Phnom Penh has increased to 3,000 tons, of which 65% is wet waste, 20% is plastic bags and more than 10% is solid waste. Only 20% of the waste is recycled, making the rate of dumping in landfills at 70 to 80%, and some of the rubbish being dumped on public roads.
Most of the waste collected by the participants was plastic bag litter, which was strewn in public squares after some locals did not properly dispose of the garbage.
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment emphasized that in the Royal Government of the sixth mandate, the Ministry of Environment has set its vision, stating that the quality and sustainability of the environment are guaranteed.
