Delegates from all countries praised Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, with respect and satisfaction for leading the country to peace, stability and rapid development, especially religious harmony. Legal Awareness: Public Offenses
Therefore, the Legal Awareness Program of FRESH NEWS, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation this week, would like to raise the topic of "Public Insult Offenses" to share with the following citizens and students:
1: Verbally, of any kind, uttered in public or during public meetings. For example, in a meeting, Mr. A used inappropriate words (supernatural words) to Mr. B and compared Mr. B to dogs and cats.
2. By letter or drawing, of any kind, which is disseminated among the public or displayed for the public to see. For example, Mr. Sok posted insulting words on his Facebook page and used a cut-out image of Mr. Sao with a dog's head and posted it publicly.
3: By all means of telecommunication, audio-visual for the public. Telecommunication and audio-visual means, such as television and radio. For example, Ms. Sros recorded her insulting remarks to Mr. Sanghar and aired the recording on a radio station. The act of insulting the press is subject to the law on the press.
According to Article 309 (Criminal Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia), the procedure for filing a defamation complaint is different from other offenses.
1. In the case of insulting a member of the government or a public official or a citizen with a public mandate, the accusation shall be made by the person concerned or by the head of the relevant institution.
2. In the case of insulting a private person, the accusation shall be made by the person who suffered the insult.
In summary, public defamation is a criminal offense, a type of misdemeanor and punishable only by a fine, and this type of offense has special procedures for filing a complaint with the plaintiff. The person or institution concerned is a necessary condition of the criminal charge and the prosecution is also dismissed by the withdrawal of the person or the institution concerned.
