For more details, please read the full announcement of the Ministry of Commerce: Elder Chapey Dong Veng, Mr. Prach Chhuon passed away
Prach Chhuon was taken by his family to Calmette Hospital for several days. The body of Prach Chhuon was taken by his family to a traditional Buddhist ceremony at Wat Botum Vatei under the auspices of Samdech Preah Moha Sometheathipady Non Nget, Samdech Preah Sang, President of the Kingdom of Cambodia and Samdech Preah Chao Athikar Wat Botum Vatey.
Prach Chhuon was born in October 1936, the youngest of six farmers in Trapeang Kak village, Khvav commune, Traing district, Takeo province. He started learning chapei at the age of nine from a paternal uncle named Pet Dup called Chang Kom Pram Bei.
He was a student of a monk named Atsen in Wat Prohm. Monks taught him Pali songs, poems and literary tales, which made him rich in rhetoric and literary knowledge.
After learning how to chase three long chapis, his first audience was the locals. Prach Chhuon is a chapei singer with a big, round voice, juicy and talented.
Later, in 1962, he participated in the National Radio's "Competition for the Selection of Chapey Dang Veng" and won the competition with the first place among the 20 participants.
After the fall of Democratic Kampuchea or the Khmer Rouge regime on January 7, 1979, Prach Chhuon resumed his career as a chapei singer. He returned to Phnom Penh and sang on the National Radio of Cambodia. In addition, he was invited to sing on other programs.
Prach Chhuon now has five children, three of whom live with him. Throughout his life, he held the chapei pole regularly. Due to his fame, he was invited to sing in some provinces and capitals, such as Ratanakiri, Mondulkiri, Oddar Meanchey and Phnom Penh.
Grandpa's songs have some humor and most of them are educational. He likes to sing about women's rules, and he knows how to sing up to four copies of Kram Ngoy. Not only that, he always composed songs immediately while singing, and the phrases also sometimes sound good. Asia-Pacific Emergency and Disaster Care Network Holds 2018 Annual Conference to Build Disaster Capacity and Resilience Involve Nurses
