WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The brightest galaxy ever discovered is engulfing at least three tiny galaxies nearby, a scientific study released on Thursday said. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Friday afternoon, November 16, 2018.
According to a study by NASA's JPL Research and Development Center, the galaxy is being stolen from its neighbors, contributing to the galaxy's illumination. Out like this.
Like the machines on Earth, its enormous emission of energy requires equal amounts of energy, and that energy comes from dust and space gases to form stars and supply clouds around the giant black hole.
The galaxy, named W2246, is the farthest galaxy that scientists have found to be absorbing matter from a variety of sources. Its light takes 12.4 billion light-years to travel to us, so scientists are seeing the galaxy in shape when our universe is only one-tenth of its 13.8 billion-year-old age. It.
