The Chinese government-sponsored Chinese Audio and Video Copyright Association has ordered karaoke clubs to remove more than 6,000 songs in a bid to crack down on intellectual property infringement in China.
In a new statement on the association's website, the karaoke production company and karaoke venue were asked to remove 6,609 music videos deemed to be in violation of Chinese intellectual property laws.
The association, established in 2008, is the only body approved by the National Intellectual Property Administration to administer the overall rights of intellectual property and other rights related to video and audio.
All karaoke shops can only use the song and music video if they have become a member of the association and paid for the intellectual property license fee only. Otherwise, they will often be sued for intellectual property infringement. This is according to Zhou Yaping, the acting director general of the association.
Zhou Yaping, acting director general of the China Audio and Video Intellectual Property Association, said: A preliminary decision calling for an end to the abuse and the removal of the songs and videos. "So we told them to remove it to encourage them to abide by the court's decision."
Zhou explained that only certain series of songs and videos were requested to be deleted. Legally licensed song series are still available as usual. He explained that although some popular songs were temporarily banned, the move was only intended to strengthen the implementation of intellectual property rights in China for the long-term benefit.
(Takeo): The National Police Commissioner General, General Dr. Neth Savoeun, on November 16, 2018, issued an order suspending the work of the Police Inspector of Daun Keo City, Takeo Province, Mr. Chhay Savuth, in connection with the violence against his wife. Body.
The move comes after Pov Sopheap, the wife of Daun Keo police chief, posted a picture of the violence of her husband, Colonel Chhay Savuth, who beat his wife while she was pursuing adultery. 10 pm, November 15.
Colonel Chhay Savuth slapped his wife in the face in front of his mistress, causing a scar on his face. Colonel Chhay Savuth admitted that he had slapped his wife, claiming that his wife had hit the back of his car, damaging it.
After posting the picture, the National Police Commissioner hurriedly issued three orders to the Takeo Provincial Police Commissioner. The three regulations include:
* First: Please suspend the work of this police inspector to investigate * Second: Check the facts and mediate with the family. If mediation is not possible, a case file must be compiled and sent to court.
China and Singapore on Monday agreed to strengthen free trade agreements and expand ties between the two countries.
This agreement, along with other agreements related to the promotion of bilateral cooperation in the field of technology development and other areas, was agreed at several meetings held in Singapore.
The agreement was reached between Chinese Premier Li Keqiang and Singaporean Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong. This is the first time a Chinese prime minister has paid an official visit to Singapore in 11 years.
The Prime Minister of Singapore said that the expansion of the free trade agreement will bring real benefits to the people and companies of the two countries, as well as show the world a strong signal that Continuing to expand trade and investment flows.
The two sides also pledged to speed up the construction of a new land and waterway economic corridor with multiple freight routes, both land and water, to connect China's central and southwestern regions with Southeast Asia.
Experts say the economic corridor will enable China to open up its markets to the world more widely and create a shortcut for Southeast Asian countries to do business in the country. China has.
At the meeting, the Chinese Prime Minister said that the two sides should actively expand cooperation on building smart cities, as well as promote the development of urban management and the development of artificial intelligence technology.
Intergovernmental cooperation projects between China and Singapore, such as the Suzhou Industrial Park Project and the Echo Tianjin City Project, have yielded fruitful results. The expansion of the Guangzhou Knowledge City project is also expected to provide new impetus to practical bilateral cooperation.
After the talks, the two leaders witnessed the signing of a number of agreements related to the expansion of free trade agreements, financial, scientific and technological ties, environment, culture and tradition. (China's CCTV)
