(Phnom Penh): At the Roundtable Meeting of the 25th National Hepatitis Control Program on the morning of November 14, 2018, Prof. Eng Huot, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health, claimed that the Ministry of Health has reduced leprosy or leprosy. No longer a public health problem, but has not yet been completely eliminated.
Prof. Eng Huot confirmed that the implementation of the activities of the National Hepatitis C Program continued in 2018, hepatitis is considered by the Ministry among many other infectious diseases in the country. The database of the Hansen National Antidote Program has been set up and launched.
He added that all registered hepatitis C cases were included in the database, which provided important information for the evaluation and management of new cases. The activities of the National Hepatitis Control Program include the management of reactions, the supply of drugs that lead to the achievement of new goals and activities in the future.
"In 2018, we will focus on continuing to fill the gaps between the community and the health system by empowering people to come for consultation, research and treatment," said the Secretary of State for Health.
Prof. Eng Huot added, "We encourage provincial-district strategies to work with volunteers, government teams and NGOs, as well as factory and media outreach campaigns."
He added that the rehabilitation unit for victims of Lansen disease, which was set up to treat patients with disabilities at the National Center for Rehabilitation of the Disabled, Kien Khleang, Phnom Penh, was working well.
In addition, there is an anesthesia department at the Battambang Provincial Hospital to treat foot ulcers caused by anesthesia and to provide restorative surgery services to patients who are progressing well. .
"We have reduced lentils to a public health problem but have not completely eliminated them. This means that transmission is low and persists, resulting in new cases occurring. "We know that the incubation phase of the disease is long, and there will be new cases for many years to come." This is the emphasis of Professor Eng Huot, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health.
At the same time, Professor Eng Huot confirmed that the Ministry of Health is still focusing on finding new cases of hepatitis and effective treatment with various drugs. Outreach to Lansen in the community and health workers at all levels plays an important role in finding new cases and treatments, especially as the prevalence rate decreases.
