Almost all rubber processing factories in Tbong Khmum province have closed due to Vietnamese traders rushing to buy and export without paying taxes to the state
(Tbong Khmum): Many handicrafts and rubber processing factories in Tbong Khmum province are facing closure while some locations have been closed for months due to Vietnamese traders coming to collect rubber trees, cashew trees and trees. Akasya smuggled out of some corridors without paying the correct tax to some states, only to pay $ 200 for distribution to the relevant authorities and officials at the border.
On November 14, 2018, the representative of the President of Tbong Khmum Rubber Processing Factory Association, Mr. Sok Leang Sren, told Fresh News that Vietnamese traders continue to collect rubber trees, cashew trees and acacia trees at high prices. Many local businesses have not been able to compete with them, and some factories have been closed for months.
His location has been closed for several months due to the inability to compete with rubber traders like Vietnamese traders, who have only recently tried to buy a few.
Regarding this issue, he stated that the reason why Vietnamese traders rushed to buy and export so loosely was because they did not pay the state, only 800,000 riel per car paid to the officials. Involved at the border, while legal traders have to pay the state through customs up to $ 1,600 per car, causing some local businesses to go bankrupt.
He said that despite the order from the Minister of Agriculture to prevent the smuggling of rubber trees, but seems to be ineffective, and recently Vietnamese traders have changed the image of the day. At night, during the day, they collect, buy and store at people's homes.
In this regard, he appealed to the National Police Commissioner General, especially Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, to take effective measures to prevent Vietnamese traders from making such a storm.
It should be noted that local rubber wood processing traders in Tbong Khmum province mentioned many difficulties and losses due to Vietnamese traders collecting rubber trees, cashew trees and acacia trees in Tbong Khmum province. Dozens of trucks without paying proper taxes to the state.
Minister of Agriculture Veng Sakhon in early October 2017 also informed the Tbong Khmum provincial governor to intervene to close the export of these raw materials, but still not effective, despite the Minister warned to take action. By that.
At the same time, the local rubber wood processing business also submitted another letter of intervention to the Tbong Khmum provincial governor for action, but still has not yet been effective.
