Samdech Techo Hun Sen Attends the Opening Ceremony of the 33rd ASEAN Summit
(Singapore): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and leaders of nine other ASEAN member countries on the afternoon of November 13, 2018, attended the opening ceremony of the 33rd ASEAN Summit under the theme "Building Innovation and Sustainability." ASEAN Strength ”held at the Sun Tech International Convention and Exhibition Center, Singapore.
The ASEAN Heads of State and Government warmly welcomed Lee Hsien Loong, Prime Minister of the Republic of Singapore and Chairman of the 33rd ASEAN Summit. At the opening ceremony of the 33rd ASEAN Summit, the Heads of State and Government of ASEAN countries paid tribute to the ASEAN national anthem and national anthem, especially the Singaporean folk art song related to ASEAN activities and celebrations.
In his keynote address at the opening of the 33rd ASEAN Summit, Li Xianlong, chair of the 33rd ASEAN Summit, emphasized that going back more than 50 years, ASEAN is now turning a new page and is moving towards Find multiple directions.
The President was very excited that during his presidency, all member countries doubled our efforts and came up with excellent solutions that show the results of unity, efficiency and alliance on behalf of ASEAN.
At the opening ceremony of the 33rd ASEAN Summit, Mr. Lee Hsien Loong, Prime Minister of the Republic of Singapore and Chairman of the 33rd ASEAN Summit, presented a prize of approximately $ 25,000 to a representative of the NGO. Keep mentally retarded children in the Philippines. ASEAN leaders also took photos of the opening ceremony of the 33rd ASEAN Summit.
It should be noted that ASEAN leaders also listened to presentations by the ASEAN Business Advisory Council and opened the 33rd ASEAN Plenary Summit and Dinner hosted by HE Lee Hsien Loong, Prime Minister. Singapore as well. APSARA Authority trains children to understand the history and conservation of archeology so that they can love and participate in protection
(Siem Reap): Banteay Samre Primary School is the second target school of the Angkor Heritage Education Program organized on November 11, 2018 by APSARA Authority with the aim of contributing to the education sector to integrate heritage knowledge, environment and skills. Reading to children living in the Angkor area.
This program shares knowledge by archaeologists of the APSARA Authority on the history, culture, traditions, beliefs, religions, environment and the role of children in contributing to the preservation and knowledge of the heritage in their area.
Therefore, cultivating children to know the history of their heritage makes them love, understand the value and participate in protection. Obviously, in neighboring countries and developed countries in the education system, this lesson has been introduced to children from a young age and they have been doing it for a long time, but our country has not done it yet.
He said that when Angkor became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, APSARA Authority also urged the APSARA Authority to consider heritage education for local students to know about their heritage.
As a first step, in 2009, APSARA Authority started to publish a picture book about heritage sculpture in collaboration with the Department of Education, Youth and Sports of Siem Reap Province and UNESCO, Japan in two parts, and to teach children about heritage with a collaborative project. With New Zealand and the Cambodian community in Canada.
Currently, APSARA Authority is also making the third level of heritage books to make students know what Angkor is and implement the heritage education program in collaboration with the Heritage Support Organization for students at Srah Srang Secondary School to prevent Cambodian students from having The gap in understanding of one's heritage is far from foreign.
Rick Reth, conservation officer of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Parks and Preventive Archeology, said that the inclusion of archeological knowledge for children is the aim of conservationists who want children to understand how to care for repair techniques and materials. And the protection of those ancestral heritage from future generations.
He wants all Cambodians to take care of all children, young people, the elderly, as well as those who enjoy the Angkor area.
Ms. Kan Kem, Deputy Director of Banteay Samre Primary School, thanked the APSARA Authority for setting up this program for the benefit of the students so that they know about the heritage where they live. In particular, can behave well to contribute to the protection when they visit the temple.
He said that in the past, such programs did not exist, but the school used to do it once when teachers wrote essays about the temple and took students to observe the archeological site. With more such programs, students will learn more about the heritage areas where they live.
