Asia Injury Prevention Foundation Distributes Over 1,000 Helmets to Families Protecting Children in Sihanoukville
(Sihanoukville): The National Road Safety Committee, with the support of Manulife, on November 12, 2018, distributed more than 1,000 helmets to students, teachers, parents and guardians. Students of Hun Sen Krong Primary School, located in Sihanoukville.
In addition, more than 100 parents and guardians will participate in a series of training workshops to promote helmet wearing, with the Helmet Program for Families focusing on the contribution of parents, guardians, especially role models. To children as children to wear helmets.
The event was attended by Ms. Mon Manvy, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary General of the National Committee for Road Safety, Deputy Governor of Sihanoukville and traffic safety stakeholders, private sector development partners, parents, guardians, students, teachers. Staff and administrative team of Hun Sen Krong Primary School, staff of Manulife Cambodia and staff of Asia Injury Prevention Foundation.
Ms. Mon Manvi, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary General of the National Road Safety Committee, stated that there is a positive link between parents' knowledge of proper helmet use and children's knowledge of helmet use. Wear a helmet properly.
Ms. Mon Manvy added that the Royal Government really supports all efforts to raise awareness of road traffic safety, especially the wearing of helmets among students to reduce and prevent deaths and injuries from traffic accidents. .
Similarly, Mr. Kim Panha, Director of the Asian Injury Prevention Foundation in Cambodia, confirmed that Hun Sen Primary School in Sihanoukville was selected to implement the program because of the high risk area and students have to travel back and forth. The school is the busiest street in the province, with about 90 percent of its students traveling by bicycle or motorbike with their parents.
He added that last year one student died and at least seven students and teachers were injured in a car accident, and the family helmet program will provide helmets with Quality and traffic safety training for students, teachers and parents of students living in communities prone to traffic accidents.
Please be informed that the helmet program for families will be implemented in Sihanoukville throughout the school year 2018 to 2019.
