(Phnom Penh): SCG, one of the leading multinational corporations in the ASEAN region, has signed a Memorandum of Understanding with the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to support the promotion of driving training knowledge for driving training centers. Heavy vehicles in Phnom Penh, which will play an important role in raising awareness of traffic laws and safe driving.
As road use increases day by day, SCG is concerned about the safety and well-being of Cambodians, especially when it comes to driving and road use. Therefore, SCG and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport have developed training programs to improve driving skills, especially for heavy vehicles.
The training will utilize SCG's skills and knowledge through its Heavy Truck Driving Training Center, which operates in Thailand. This training program will benefit the driver both avoiding accidents and performing proper management of his vehicle.
"With good driving skills, the roads in Cambodia can be safer than before. We would like to thank the Ministry of Public Works and Transport for giving us the opportunity to participate as part of the training," he said. The duration of the signing of the Memorandum of Understanding today. "This agreement will allow us to work closely together to raise the standard of driving in Cambodia."
Meanwhile, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, stated that the training will take place at the Heavy Truck Driving Training Center in Russey Keo District, Phnom Penh. This training center will play an important role in raising awareness. Of drivers around traffic laws, thus will contribute to reducing traffic accidents in Cambodia.
"I would like to thank SCG for training our trainers in Thailand, and the trainers will be coming to teach our officers more to help train heavy truck drivers," he said.
In 2017, a traffic accident caused by a heavy truck claimed the lives of nearly 200 people in Cambodia. According to the senior minister, 230,000 trucks are currently registered, but the level of training and licensing is still low, of which only 60% are heavy truck drivers. Have a valid driver's license.
Road accidents in Cambodia kill about five people and injure between 15 and 20 people every day, costing Cambodia hundreds of millions of dollars in property damage, treatment and loss of productivity. This training is part of a solution that will reduce the accident rate and make driving on the road safer.
