(Paris): Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, on November 11, 2018, led a high-level Cambodian delegation to celebrate the 100th anniversary of the end of the First World War. The Paris Peace Forum commemorates and pays tribute to the souls of Cambodian heroes who sacrificed their lives in the cause of the struggle with the French army and people for the end of World War I in 1918.
After Cambodia gained independence in 1953 under the Royal Crusade of King Norodom Sihanouk, Cambodia enjoyed peace but lost due to the civil war since 1970. In 1979, the Democratic Kampuchea regime collapsed, but the Khmer Rouge's political and military organization had not yet been completely eradicated.
The Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991 led to a general election organized by the United Nations in 1993, but the civil war continued. During that time, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, pursued a win-win policy until the establishment of the historic event on December 29, 1998, when the last political and military organization of the Khmer Rouge was completely dissolved.
Cambodia has regained national unity, national unity, territorial unity and complete peace under the same constitutional roof, which this poor homeland has lost for more than 400 years. This peace is the foundation for the Cambodian people to move forward on the path to respect for human rights, the rule of law and democracy, economic development, poverty reduction and prosperity, making a significant contribution to regional peace and stability. And the world.
The motherland and the people of Cambodia have been enjoying and fully supporting the current policy of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is maintaining and safeguarding peace and political stability to promote sustainable development and harmonious living. Of the Cambodian people.
