"People used to be divided, but today we come together to celebrate the inauguration," he said. And I believe that the souls of our Cambodian ancestors are also happy to see that their children are not divided, not killed by war, and together build more achievements.
Mr. HUN Manet emphasized that the inauguration of achievements in the field of religion is very important, if a society without religion is difficult to live without harmony, it is destructive and destructive like in the Pol Pot era. But after 1979, we were prosperous and the religious sector contributed a lot to nation building.
He added, "If we do not have peace of mind, no morality, we are constantly fighting, and if we do not have ideas to share and without participation, we can not build this achievement. I would like to thank all the monks and thank the people who have built more achievements to meet the needs of Buddhism.
"Together we are rich, the country is peaceful, we have the right to freedom of movement, there is respect for all religions, and that is what the people want," he said. In the Pol Pot era, there was nothing. And what the people want is for the country to be led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen to be happier, to solve what the people want.
He said that the leadership of the country is unstoppable, we build the country from scratch, we build together gradually until now. Importantly, the Royal Government has created all kinds of conditions to improve the living standards of the people, civil servants, create various job opportunities for the children of the people. This is to lead the country in celebrating with the people.
Please be informed that Koh Joram Pagoda is one of the 41 pagodas in the district, located in Leu village, Koh Joram commune, Khsach Kandal district, Kandal province, with a land size of 2 hectares, 30 km from Phnom Penh and 40 km from the capital. Kandal Province. The pagoda was first built in 1831 by the Buddhists, a movement united with the monks.
Until now, Koh Joram pagoda is 187 years old, having passed through 7 chief priests. During the genocidal regime of Pol Pot (April 17, 1975 - January 7, 1979), pagodas, cultural infrastructure, traditions, and religious beliefs were completely destroyed.
Separately, Koh Joram pagoda was closed by the Khmer Rouge and turned into a deserted desert, in which the monks in the pagoda were decapitated and forced to work hard, and the achievements were almost completely destroyed.
