Mr. Prosithon is a lawyer originally from Dey Krahorm village, Prey Romdeng commune, Kirivong district, Takeo province, the third child in a family of four. Today he is married and has a daughter, the bond of a happy marriage.
Mr. Prosithon is currently the Senior Managing Director of the Legal and Legal Operations Department of Manulife and is a volunteer with the position of Vice President of the JICA Cambodia Alumni Association. The former civil servant of the Office of the Council of Ministers is also a professor of law at the Royal University of Law and Economics.
Prositon's fame has caught the attention of the media. In early November 2018, Fresh News sought out this lawyer for an interview in order to share his life experience with Cambodian children as a model for future use.
According to the biography of this Takeo resident, he has a father who is a teacher and a mother who is a salesman and also works as a farmer. As a child, Prosithon always worked hard to study and help with family chores in his spare time, and in those days the peasants were not very wealthy.
"Most of the time, from work to study, we work for my parents, my mother is a salesman, so I help him with his work," he said. In the morning, help pull the cart and go. At 11:00, we collect the goods to go home.
After finishing 12th grade, he came to live with his uncle in Phnom Penh to study law at Norton University and passed the exam as a civil servant at the Council of Ministers. He wanted to study abroad, but his scholarship failed miserably.
Although the countless failures did not cause Prositon to lose his efforts. Until 2008, through JICA, he was awarded a scholarship in international business law to study in Japan, which is when he was associated with this organization.
After graduating from Japan, he also volunteered for the JICA Cambodia Alumni Association, which has more than 1,000 members, which was founded by a group of Japanese graduates. He has also been called up by Manulife to this day as the Senior Managing Director of the Legal and Legal Operations Department.
"Since the bankruptcy, I have always been interested in the subject of the Buk (Scholarship), among them, the Japanese Buk, I am more interested in," he said. They. In other words, I was exhausted and I struggled, I did not give up easily.
In his role as head of a large department, Prositon has been hailed by his descendants as an intelligent and talented leader. He is a person who is flexible in all situations to solve problems.
"For me, I have known Sithon for three years. When I started working with him, I was impressed by his different leadership, he always gave his children opportunities. So he always advises his subordinates to stand out and work hard. ”
Mr. Prosithon also intends to be a person who can contribute to the development of Cambodia to grow further. He said that helping to promote the nation is the responsibility of everyone in the way they can.
