(China): Many online hospitals provide better health care to patients from rural areas through telemedicine. The hospital has been in a pilot project in the eastern Chinese city of Wuzhen since 2015.
In 2015, the Chinese government launched a number of Internet pilot projects aimed at improving people's living conditions through the use of online technology. The Internet Hospital in Wuxi is considered one of the successes of these projects. It has now become an important system providing remote medical services.
The 33-year-old Ly Rong has been receiving treatment for a brain tumor for 15 years. He later developed kidney problems and a blockage in a blood vessel in his brain. Li lives in a poor area in northwest China's Shaanxi Province. His family could not afford better medical bills.
Lee was initially treated at a hospital in his hometown. And because his condition was so complicated, the hospital asked for help from Wuxin Internet Hospital. Wuzhen Hospital soon contacted experts from senior health institutions in other provinces. Later, they also planned to treat patients using remote therapy.
In particular, Zhang Chunhua, director of the Wuxin Internet Hospital, said: "Remote treatment services greatly reduce patients' travel costs. "It also helps local doctors by developing their skills in the treatment of diseases."
Wuxi Internet Hospital connects with more than 2,700 hospitals and more than 260,000 doctors across China, and has set up 12 remote treatment centers for specific ailments to help patients across the country. Countries, especially those living in remote areas, have access to quality medical care.
Liao Jiayuan, founder of Wuzhen Internet Hospital, said: "The main purpose of setting up this online treatment plan is to break down existing barriers in the hospital to enable patients in rural and remote areas to access medical services. Quality. "Internet hospitals can also play a major role in balancing health resources and assisting local health institutions to increase their efficiency." (China's CCTV)
(China): China wants to further strengthen bilateral trust with ASEAN as China celebrates the 15th anniversary of the establishment of a long-term partnership between China and ASEAN. This is according to Chinese Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Hua Chhunying on Tuesday.
Hour Chhun Ying said some of the events held this year reflected new opportunities for cooperation between East Asian countries.
"This year marks the 15th anniversary of the establishment of a long-term partnership between China and ASEAN, as well as the year of China-ASEAN innovation," she said. Sino-Japanese-Korean cooperation is on the rise. Negotiations on a comprehensive regional economic partnership agreement are moving fast. "And countries in the region also hope to continue to integrate into the region."
Chinese Premier Li Keqiang will pay a state visit to Singapore and attend a meeting of leaders for East Asian cooperation from November 12.
"Prime Minister Li Keqiang's participation in this year's East Asia Cooperation Leaders' Meeting is very important for the promotion and development of China-China relations," she said. ASEAN also promotes the deep development of East Asian regional cooperation. "Currently, the situation in the world and in the region is in a state of turmoil and profound change."
"As a developing country, China and other ASEAN countries have many similar interests and there are strong hopes for stronger cooperation," she said.
"We would like to take this opportunity to mark the 15th anniversary of the establishment of the China-ASEAN long-term partnership to further strengthen bilateral political trust and deepen cooperation in the field," said a Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman. "Economy and trade, innovation, cultural and people-to-people exchanges, the military, as well as boosting bilateral ties to a higher level."
Hua said China hopes the East Asia Cooperation Leaders' Meeting in Singapore will contribute to regional development.
