(Svay Rieng): Ms. Mom Phen, a 39-year-old widow with two dependent children, now lives in Trok village, Ta Suos commune, Romeas Hek district, Svay Rieng province. Chickens and ducks for sale were able to withstand the cost, but raising his chickens and ducks did not yield good results, as there were almost no deaths during the disease.
But to date, following the EU project in the field of animal husbandry implemented by Veterinary Without Borders (AVSE), which teaches how to vaccinate and care for chickens so that they can avoid diseases. He was able to get a better life by earning a decent income from selling his chickens and ducks.
She said that until she got enough space to raise chickens and ducks, she had spent about 8 million riels, and in doing so, there was also the help of Veterinarian Without Borders, which helped to set up a net around it. Help as a chicken breed and some vaccines, in addition to raising this chicken, he also received training in raising this animal often.
She said that in her place, she raises chickens, ducks, ducks, broilers, catfish, catfish, trout, as well as growing more vegetables, and the income from the sale of chickens and ducks may be enough to cover the cost of food. The child's education and expenses, and there are some leftovers that can help his family's life better.
It should be noted that the EU project in the field of animal husbandry aims to build and strengthen the skills, knowledge and management capacity of the animal health agency and promote the veterinary services provided by community agencies, and this project focuses on skills. And the operation of the village animal health agency so that the agency can respond to the needs of the animal husbandry community as well.
The project uses a variety of approaches, in which stakeholders such as civil society organizations, village health agencies, animal husbandry communities, traders, local authorities come together to share experiences and network to promote animal health services and livestock products. The project is also to strengthen technical and business skills, as well as the cooperation of the Veterinary Health Agency, which leads to more revenue.
