(Phnom Penh): After concluding her 11-day visit to Cambodia on the afternoon of November 8, 2018, Ms. Rona Smith, UN Special Rapporteur, issued a statement concluding her mission on the human rights situation in Cambodia. Cambodia.
The following is Rona Smith's statement:
Nationwide Traffic Law Enforcement Campaign on November 08: 8,084 illegal vehicles found and 1,024 fined
(Phnom Penh): The nationwide traffic law enforcement campaign for November 08, 2018, the traffic police force inspected 8,084 vehicles and found 1,218 vehicles violating the law (81% motorcycles), 194 education vehicles and park 1,024 fines, according to the official report of the Traffic Police and Public Order Department of the General Commission of the National Police.
According to the report, 2,994 helmets and goggles were inspected on November 8, 164 education helmets and 929 glasses were fined.
For the results of the 8-day inspection from November 1 to 8, the police inspected 39,468 vehicles and found 8,073 offending vehicles (79% motorcycles), including education 1, 337 units and fined 6,736 units. Ms. Rona Smith considers the OWSO at the sub-national level as an important step in accessing effective services for citizens.
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Rona Smith, UN Special Rapporteur on Human Rights in Cambodia, said that the implementation of the OWSO at the sub-national level and the receipt of complaints from the people are important steps in Receive effective and highly accountable services at the sub-national level.
In a press conference at the Office of Human Rights in Cambodia to present the preliminary report of the 11-day visit to Cambodia on the afternoon of November 8, 2018, Ms. Rona Smith also welcomed the Royal Government. As well as the Ministry of Interior, which has established this one-stop service.
"I welcome the OWSO at the sub-national level, and in particular the establishment of a Citizens' Office to receive complaints and public services," the UN Special Rapporteur said. "These are important steps in accessing effective and highly accountable services at the sub-national level."
In addition, Ms. Rona Smith also pointed out that she met with the Minister of Economy and Finance regarding the work of budget management and the requirements for other ministries to submit a clear budget for their expenditures. Encouraging.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng stressed that the OWSO also has a place to receive complaints from people to address what is inactive in providing services to people at the local level.
At this point, Ms. Rona Smith also expressed her congratulations and encouraged the government as well as the Ministry of Interior to continue to work hard to strengthen the establishment of this OWSO to serve the people more easily and Closer to the people.
Rona Smith Welcomes Government to Raise Funds for Legal Aid to the Poor (Video Inside) (Phnom Penh): UN Special Rapporteur on Human Rights Rona Smith in Cambodia on the afternoon of November 8. In 2018, in the press conference after the 11-day visit to Cambodia, expressed welcome to the Royal Government to increase the budget for legal aid to the poor.
Ms. Rona Smith also encouraged the Royal Government to increase the number of legal aid to the poor to serve all those in need in the near future.
"I also welcome the increase in funding for legal aid to the poor, and I encourage further increase in legal aid to the poor," he said. More to serve all those who need it.
She emphasized that the availability of legal aid for those in need was an important step in ensuring access to justice for all, and in this case, progress in the formulation of legal aid policies for the people. Poverty through a participatory and participatory design process is gaining traction as she awaits early adoption of the policy.
In addition, Ms. Rona Smith emphasized that the right to a fair trial also requires an expedited hearing, and she is concerned about the timing of the trial and the sheer number of detainees in the prison.
She wants to use provisional detention only when absolutely necessary, in accordance with the Code of Criminal Procedure and carefully compiling the use of provisional detention, and regularly reviewing the need to use provisional detention. In each case.
