China's ninth Arctic ice explorer team completed a 69-day exploration mission recently. They turned the page on the history of the use of a new device used to capture signals.
The Sailong Ice Transporter, which means Ice Dragon, carried more than 130 researchers and traveled more than 23,150 km during the search. The 7,037-kilometer road crosses the ice in the north.
The submarine team completed its first scientific investigation in the Bering Sea on Sept. 10, recording temperatures, salinity and depths at more than 200 locations.
"We have gathered a lot of data that forms a solid foundation for our research on the mass of water and the overall flow of the seabed," said Jin Hongxier, a researcher at the 9th Arctic Exploration Team. "Bering, especially the flow of water along the edge of the peninsula."
Explorers are also investigating carbon emissions at different levels of carbon buried in the oceans in the western part of the Arctic Ocean, with three gauges mounted on the coast. Chukchi, located between Russia and the United States.
"After analysis, we can conclude that there is a strong movement of carbon that stretches across the peninsula in the western part of the The Arctic Ocean.
Carbon movements across the peninsula indicate that carbon stockpiles in the western part of the Arctic Ocean are rising against global warming. Although China has no borders with the Arctic, China has been actively involved in discussions on future development of transportation and mining.
In January, the Chinese government released its Arctic policy, emphasizing China's belief that the Arctic is an attractive location for the entire international community. The Chinese government has also set China's goals and position for the use, development and exploration of the Arctic. (China's CCTV)
