China's largest coal-fired city has banned the burning of coal in urban areas in an effort to improve air quality there. .
"I use a heating fan for the winter months," said Yao Jinlian, a resident of Ta Thong. It's good to use. The coal-free zone of Datong covers an area of more than 100 square kilometers. "The sale and use of coal is banned in the area and only a few power plants are allowed to burn coal."
In Datong, it is not new. Coal use has been limited in towns for some time, and during the past five years, nearly 1,000 coal-fired generators have been removed. Due to the limited amount of coal allowed to burn, more sites are being incorporated into a central heating community during the winter months.
"We have a centralized community heating system," said Zhang Hai, chief engineer of the Datong Environmental Protection Department. In urban areas, there is a high rate of use of this system. "We use heat radiated from heaters and power generators to supply gas to the people."
Although coal-fired power plants are still allowed to produce heat for use in central heating systems, they must comply with minimum emission standards through the use of new technologies. . This has helped to reduce the number of small particles that are harmful to people in the area. That element is called PM 2.5.
In "no-coal" areas, some factories have switched to coal-fired burners and switched to gas-fired ones. But even with the government's help, the factories still feel that the gas burners are a burden to them.
