(Siem Reap): The APSARA Authority, through the Department of Forest Management, Landscape, Culture and Environment, has planted saplings in front of Angkor Wat to replace the old trees that fell last October and to preserve the cultural landscape in the Angkor resort.
This activity took place on the morning of November 6, 2018. Deputy Director General of the APSARA Authority, Mr. Hang Pov, who was involved in planting seedlings at that time, said that the principle of the APSARA Authority is to maintain the cultural landscape in the Angkor area to remain the same as in the past.
He said that in the past, the APSARA Authority has done a lot of work related to this issue, such as treating old trees, wishing them to continue to live to preserve the cultural landscape in the area.
The replacement trees on the north side of Angkor Wat are now replacing the old ones that fell during October. And this tree is a big tree, it is 15 years old. At this point, Mr. Hang Pov explained that it was necessary to plant a large tree to be similar to the previous tree that was lost.
According to Hang Pov, the cultural landscape in the Angkor resort is related to two factors: trees and water. In this work, APSARA Authority has done a lot of work such as re-watering more water in the pond, preserving old trees and replanting in Angkor resort.
In that, Mr. Hang Pov confirmed that the forest of Angkor resort has a total of 183 species, and those species are planted and distributed by the APSARA Authority to people both inside and outside the province to plant.
