China's Ministry of Ecology and Environment will launch new measures to improve air quality as part of efforts to combat the effects of climate change.
From 2005 to 2017, China reduced its carbon emissions by 46% in one economic activity. This result is in line with the Chinese government's goal of reducing it by 50% by 2020. According to the Chinese Ministry of Environment.
To address this, the Chinese Ministry of Environment has announced new measures to further reform environmental protection, strengthen regulatory oversight and increase construction capacity in the carbon market.
At the 2015 United Nations Climate Change Conference in Paris, President Xi Jinping pledged to strengthen cooperation with the southern countries on climate change.
The ministry said 29 developing countries are actively promoting energy-saving mechanisms such as solar and solar power systems to reduce carbon emissions.
This year's climate change conference will take place in Poland in December. And Li Kao, director of the Climate Change Department of the Chinese Ministry of Ecology and Environment, described China's intention for countries attending the conference to push for a global commitment to reducing carbon emissions. In the atmosphere.
