(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment on November 6, 2018 issued an official press release confirming that the Snuol Stung Sen area of the Tonle Sap Lake Biosphere Reserve is an important wetland that has just been recognized internationally as "Ramsar site."
According to the Ministry of Environment, the Snuol Stung Sen area of the Tonle Sap Lake Biosphere Reserve, a freshwater wetland with good floodplain resources and a total area of 9,293 hectares, is recognized as an internationally important wetland. "Ramsar site" recognized by sub-decree of the Royal Government of Cambodia dated November 2, 2018.
The Stung Sen Ramsar site, located southeast of the Tonle Sap Lake, is a type of freshwater wetland rich in floodplain, long-lived and an important habitat for all kinds of biodiversity. In addition, the bushes and grasslands in the Stung Sen Ramsar site are very important in supporting birds, aquatic animals, mammals and many other species of fish.
