(Siem Reap): Residents of Nokor Krao village, Kork Chak commune, Siem Reap city, praised the construction of a new sluice gate at their base, built by the Apsara Authority. This sluice can supply water for the farms of more than 500 families on an area of nearly 100 hectares.
The Ta Sut sluice gate was completed in October after construction began in June. This sluice gate is built with the right technology, with a sluice shut-off system to trap water for use in the dry season as well.
As for Ms. Lay Tum, she is busy with the watermelon crop that is gradually rising. She said that since the water from the new sluice gate built by the APSARA Authority, it has been easier for her to draw water to irrigate crops, which before use had to hit wells and cost a lot of resources. Now Ms. Tum can easily pump water to irrigate crops by simply putting a pump out of the canal.
Mr. Phoeun Sokhim, Deputy Director of the Management Department of APSARA Authority, said that in addition to serving agricultural products to the people, the sluice gate was built to manage and store water to maintain the balance of the temple because water is very related. Close to the temple found in the Angkor resort.
According to Mr. Sokhim, this sluice can hold water for the benefit of more than 500 families on nearly 100 hectares of agricultural land. In addition, the sluice plays an important role in relieving flooding in Angkor and Siem Reap, as well as to conserve groundwater in the Angkor area.
