In response, the Minister of Agriculture stated one by one as follows:
Meanwhile, Cambodia received 16.38 million tons of mixed crops and industrial crops in 2017. All of these results have contributed to the transformation of Cambodia from a low-income country to a low-middle-income country, and there is strong hope that Cambodia will become a high-middle-income country by the end of the year. 2030.
Third, the progress of economic land concession development In the past, the Royal Government has granted economic land concessions to 330 companies. Having seen some inactivity that has arisen from the implementation of ELCs, the Royal Government has decided to revoke 101 ELC investment projects, confiscating 748,064 hectares of land.
Separately, about 619,507 hectares of land confiscated and left over from the people were set up by the Ministry as a forest extension and rehabilitation station.
Fourth, the balance of conservation of natural resources and community forestry and agriculture: In mid-2016, the head of the Royal Government decided to transfer all forest conservation work to the Ministry of Environment, which covers 2,636,080 hectares. Separately, the management of land concessions for the cultivation of all agro-industrial crops has been handed over to the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries for management.
(Phnom Penh): The US Embassy in Phnom Penh, in collaboration with the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, will sign two agreements on cultural cooperation in Cambodia, which will be held at 3:30 pm on November 6. 2018 tomorrow at the National Museum of Cambodia. This is according to a press release from the US Embassy, obtained by Fresh News today.
According to a statement from the US Embassy, the first agreement is to renew the Memorandum of Understanding between the United States Government and the Royal Government of Cambodia on "Restrictions on the Importation of Cambodian Antiquities" for a period of five years. 2018-2023.
It is a reaffirmation of the Memorandum of Understanding signed in 2003, which was renewed through the Exchange of Letters in 2008 and amended and renewed by the Exchange of Letters in 2013.
The US Embassy added that the Memorandum of Understanding aims to reduce the incidence of looting of artifacts that represent Cambodia's precious and rich cultural heritage, which is irreplaceable.
Cambodia will continue its best efforts to maintain and share information with the United States Government on unauthorized mining, theft of cultural property, illegal trade in cultural property, and other threats that may cause Danger to Cambodia's cultural heritage.
The second agreement is a grant agreement from the Ambassador Fund for Cultural Preservation 2018, approved between the US State Department and the US Embassy in Phnom Penh to support the Preah Vihear National Authority with $ 200,000. It is called "Preservation of the ancient staircase on the north side of the temple, which dates back to the 11th century."
