Understand the law: the difference between fraud and breach of trust
Before exploring the differences between the two crimes, please reconsider the definition of fraud and breach of trust.
1. Fraud is an act of fraud against a natural person or legal entity by using a false name, claiming false status, infringing on real status, or using fraudulent tactics and thus receiving from this person which leads to damage. The interests of this person or third parties:
Assignment of funds, values or assets. - Provision of services. - Obligatory letter making or obligatory release.
Example: Mr. A tells Mr. B that he is a senior official in an institution if Mr. B wants to work for this institution. He was able to help Mr. B by requiring him to hand over $ 5,000. But obviously, he is just a contracted officer. Therefore, Mr. A's actions are an imitation of status in order to receive value or property from Mr. A.
