Minister of Agriculture meets with Dean of the Faculty of Bio-Agriculture, Japan on doctoral training in agriculture
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, on the afternoon of November 1, 2018, received Prof. Kazuhito Kawakita, Dean of the Faculty of Biological Agriculture of Nagoya University, Japan and his colleagues for a courtesy call. Inform the activities of cooperation between Nagoya University and Royal University of Agriculture on doctoral training in agriculture.
On that occasion, Professor Kawakita informed the Minister about the second phase of the plan to strengthen and expand cooperation between the two sides, in addition to doctoral training, Nagoya University also plans to continue to establish trilateral cooperation between Cambodia and Japan. Philippines by establishing an Agricultural Science Center in the tropics to further strengthen Cambodia's educational and research capabilities.
At the same time, Minister Veng Sakhon expressed his deep gratitude to Nagoya University for its active participation in promoting human resources in Cambodia, and he also highly appreciated the activities as well as the good cooperation. Better between all stakeholders.
In response, Professor Kawakita vowed that the University of Nagoya will examine and do its best to help the Ministry's professional staff increase their capacity to help develop agriculture, especially the nation.
Please be informed that the excellent cooperation between Nagoya University and Royal University of Agriculture started in 2000. Noting that the cooperation between the two sides has achieved remarkable results and still has the potential to expand, Nagoya University has decided to establish three Satellite Institute in Cambodia.
These include: 1: Royal University of Agriculture for studying and promoting agriculture, 2: Royal University of Phnom Penh for studying international development, 3: Royal University of Law and Economics for studying law.
What should be proud of for the Royal University of Agriculture and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in 2017 and 2018, two professional officers successfully completed their doctoral studies. Currently, there are still 5 professional officers studying for doctoral degrees.
