(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ang Vong Vathana, Minister of Justice, on the afternoon of November 1, 2018, presided over the transfer and inauguration ceremony of the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court. The ceremony was held at the Phnom Penh Municipal Court with the participation of many other stakeholders.
It should be noted that Mr. Yet Chakrya was ordered by His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni Tras to transfer his position from the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court to the Deputy Attorney General of the Supreme Court. As for Mr. Keo Sothea, he was transferred by the King from the Prosecutor of the Kampong Speu Provincial Prosecutor's Office to the Prosecutor of the Phnom Penh Municipal Prosecutor's Office, replacing Mr. Yet Chakrya.
New report: Natural areas around the world are rapidly disappearing due to human activities
(London): The Nature magazine has called on the entire international community to take immediate action to protect natural areas, as many rare natural areas are rapidly disappearing. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Thursday afternoon, November 1, 2018.
"A century ago, only 15 percent of the natural area was used for animal husbandry and crops ... "Currently, up to 70 percent of natural areas, including Antarctica, are being affected by human activity."
Looking back at the sea, according to satellite imagery of the four oceans, a total of 87 percent of the sea is being controlled by human activities, such as fishing, mining, pollution and transportation. It almost extends to the two poles of the earth.
For areas that can be protected, it is only if it is within the mechanism of protection by international law or it is a necessary area for all those countries themselves that the authorities make efforts to maintain. That is why all conservationists and environmentalists are calling for this issue to be raised at the forthcoming UN General Assembly.
Between 1993 and 2009, a natural area larger than India, which covered 3.3 million square kilometers, was lost to human activities such as shelter construction, agriculture, mining and other pressures. More. All of these activities are also shrinking habitats for both terrestrial and aquatic animals.
(Mondulkiri): The case of the brutal murder of an 11-year-old boy in Koh Nhek district, Mondulkiri province, with the killer cutting, slicing and cutting his abdomen until November 1 is still a mystery. The competent police of Mondulkiri province have cooperated with the local police and are continuing the investigation process.
This incident happened on October 28, 2018 at the point of the third canal near Oprang in Sre Huy village, Sre Huy commune, Koh Nhek district, Mondulkiri province.
Koh Nhek District Police Inspector said that the body was found in this case at around 6:00 pm on October 28, the victim was cut on the cheek, pierced the abdomen and cut on the head, face almost invisible.
The district police chief also confirmed that the case was suspected of murder. Currently, the authorities are investigating to arrest the perpetrators and punish them according to the law.
In this incident, the news from the villagers there were whispering that the parents of the child victim were from a family linked to witchcraft.
Sre Huy village chief Krak Toi also said that the family of the young victim above was claimed by the villagers here that there was a link to witchcraft, but especially when the murder occurred, the village chief This picture also can not conclude that caused by anything. He said the dead boy was a fourth grader who, in addition to being a herdsman, was herding cattle and selling doves.
Mondulkiri Provincial Commissioner Lor Sokha confirmed to Fresh News Agency in Mondulkiri province on the afternoon of November 1 that no matter what the murder case involved, he was ordering the provincial specialized police force to cooperate with Local police are investigating to find the killer and bring him to justice in the near future.
Chinese Buddhist Roundtable hopes Buddhists will work together to maintain peace in South China Sea
(Xinhua) - Senior Minister and Minister of Cult and Religion Him Chhem on November 1, 2018, attended the 3rd South China Sea Roundtable on 2018. Nannai Auditorium, Shenzhen, People's Republic of China
The event was also attended by senior monks of both denominations of the Kingdom of Cambodia, as well as representatives of the Buddhist community and senior government officials from China, Sri Lanka, Laos, Cambodia, Nepal, and Myanmar. , Mongolia, and the United States as well.
Countries in the South China Sea will help resolve conflicts and maintain peace by working together to enhance people-to-people ties with the countries of the South China Sea, which are considered the core areas of the South China Sea. Buddhism on the development of geographical location and culture. People of different nations also share a common goal to promote spiritual faith in the pursuit of a better life.
The Buddha is compassionate and seeks to heal all people from misery and seek prosperity for all and seeks harmony and the development of intimacy and openness with others in the local Buddhist community. South China Sea between people and people.
The South China Sea Buddhist Conference is committed to bringing together consensus among the people of the Buddhist countries, creating a forum for international exchange and cooperation through seminars and practical cooperation. This year's roundtable will also be a consensus that emphasizes the core values of mutual respect, justice and win-win cooperation as agreed by all parties.
The South China Sea Buddhism Roundtable hopes that all Buddhists will seek common opportunities to maintain peace, defend the South China Sea and share hardship, happiness for peace, stability and prosperity in the sea. South China.
