General Vong Pisen: The Cambodian and Vietnamese armies continue to cooperate well based on the principles that have been signed in the past.
(Phnom Penh): General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, said that the Cambodian and Vietnamese armies continue to cooperate well based on the principles that have been signed in the past, including joint patrols. Land and sea to jointly crack down on transnational crime, such as human trafficking, drug trafficking, arms trafficking.
Remarks by the Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces during his three-day official visit to Hanoi, Socialist Republic of Vietnam from October 29 to 31, 2018.
During the visit, General Vong Pisen continued to meet with the Prime Minister of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam Nguyen Sun Fuc, General Ngo Sun Lech, Minister of Defense and Lieutenant General Fan Van Yang, Chief of the General Staff of the People's Army. Vietnam at the K-2000 International Meeting Room in Hanoi.
General Vong Pisen continued that Cambodia will further strengthen the friendly relations, solidarity, brotherhood and peaceful cooperation that our two countries, Cambodia and Vietnam, have a long tradition and even closer. The purpose of this visit is to exchange experiences and strengthen cooperation and strengthen the bonds of solidarity between our two armies and the two peoples of Vietnam and Cambodia.
The Reserve Lieutenant General said that the trainees of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) who are studying in Vietnam have seen a lot of changes, and each trainee has worked hard to study and see that the quality has improved. The lieutenant general also urged the two armies, especially the young officers, to continue to exchange experiences with each other to deepen mutual trust.
In a meeting with the Vietnamese Minister of Defense, General Ngo Sun Lech recalled the memories of participating in overthrowing and preventing the return of the Khmer Rouge regime in the 1980s.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Justice, relevant institutions, on October 31, 2018, held a meeting to review the qualifications of 523 prisoners, including 37 women, proposed by prisons across the country to prepare for the release and Forgiveness in the upcoming Royal Water Festival.
This meeting was held at the Ministry of Justice on October 31, 2018, chaired by Mr. Ang Vong Vathana, Minister of Justice and Chairman of the National Commission to review the list of commutations and pardons of prisoners with the participation of Mr. Pao Hamphan, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior, Mr. Ouk Savuth, Attorney General of the Court of Appeal and some relevant officials.
Mr. Soch Sophannara, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Prosecution and Criminal Affairs, told the media that there were 523 inmates requested to be examined and considered, including 37 women from 24 prisons. Provinces, cities and 4 correctional centers as well.
Mr. Soch Sophannara said, "Today's meeting is to review the request for commutation of sentences related to the Water Festival. 24 capitals, provinces and cities and the "4 M", which we call the 4 correctional centers, and for this request, we see that there are 523 inmates across the country who apply for the 24 capitals of the 4 M, including 37 women as well.
This was stated by Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey, Director of the Private Security Management Department, on behalf of Lt. Gen. Dy Vichea, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police and Director of the Central Security Department, at the Closing Ceremony of the 8th Private Security Agency Training Course on the evening of October 31, 2018, attended by nearly 200 participants.
Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharaksmey stated, "Private security service providers and unlicensed enterprises will continue to illegally recruit forces and the National Police will take legal action in accordance with the sub-decree on private security management." Dated May 31, 2013. "
According to the director of the department, according to the above sub-decree, there will be a fine of 20 million riels for private security companies that do not seek permission from the Ministry of Interior and a fine of 10 million riels for any enterprise that chooses an agent. Private security did not ask for permission from the Capital-Provincial Hall and then build a case to be sent to court legally.
In the face of this punishment, the General Commission of the National Police, which is headed by the Department of Private Security Management, has called on all private security companies and enterprises to seek the principles and permission of the relevant institutions before proceeding.
In addition, Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharaksmey also instructed private security companies to provide services and enterprises to send their private security agents for training at the request of the Department of Private Security Management in accordance with the sub-decree of all private security agencies. Must be trained by the General Commission of the National Police.
The Royal Government is considering including three more ceremonies for the release and pardon of prisoners
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia is considering incorporating three more major festivals to reduce and pardon prisoners. This was confirmed by Mr. Soch Sophannara, Deputy Director General of the General Department of Prosecution and Criminal Affairs, Ministry of Justice on October 31, 2018.
This confirmation was made after the meeting to review the request for relaxation and pardon of 523 people for the Royal Water Festival, Floating Lanterns, Moon Worship and Ok Ambok, chaired by Mr. Ang Vong Vathana, Minister of Justice and Chairman The National Commission for Pardon and Mitigation on the afternoon of October 31, 2018.
Mr. Soch Sophannara confirmed that "this year is a very special year that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia asked the Ministry of Justice to review three more ceremonies."
He added that, however, not all prisoners who comply with the above conditions should be commuted and pardoned, but the National Commission, headed by Minister Ang Vong Vathana, should pay the most attention to further examinations. To the amendments of the inmates in the prison attached to the relevant documents, especially the verdict or final verdict.
It should be noted that currently, Cambodia has commutations and pardons for prisoners 3 times a year, including: Khmer New Year, Pisak Bochea and Water Festival, floating lanterns, worshiping the moon and Ok Ambok.
