The Royal Government of Cambodia, which consists of the National Bank of Cambodia, the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia and the National Police Commissioner General, has issued a proclamation banning people from trading in e-money as it could cause property loss while regulatory regulations are not yet in place. Yes.
To prevent this, the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Securities and Exchange Commission, plans to prepare regulations governing electronic asset trading. This is according to Mr. Sou Socheat, Director General of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia, told Fresh News.
Mr. Sou Socheat said, "The Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia, the technical officer, has given this type of asset an electronic asset, which is a type of asset that can be exchanged for cash, but not something that can be used for cash."
"Regarding e-assets, the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia is considering a regulation governing the trading of assets, as Kana is an asset that can be traded," he said. "Some countries in the region allow the creation of electronic assets. The creation of this property is called ICO, which means the creation of digital assets."
He added that currently Cambodia does not have regulations governing the trading of electronic assets, so the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia has cooperated with the National Bank of Cambodia, Ministry of Interior through the National Police issued a joint statement that because But Cambodia does not yet have all the regulations that can guarantee the trading capacity and allow companies that want to create this property.
He added that when the Securities and Exchange Commission of Cambodia has a safety net as a regulation in all aspects, it will be allowed to be used officially.
