Authorities in the northern Chinese city of Tianjin have stepped up efforts to deal with illegally imported solid waste being stored at ports.
At Tianjin Port, there are many containers for various kinds of solid waste such as plastic and paper scraps, zinc oxide and cotton scraps. This solid waste container covers an area of up to 15,000 square meters.
The buyers of these wastes left these containers because they did not dare to take responsibility for these illegal wastes after the customs unit did not issue an import permit.
It is not possible to send the illegal waste back immediately to the country where it was sent. Therefore, it may pollute the environment around the area. This is according to Tianjin Customs.
"After being left for a long time, some of the containers may have popped up because some of them did not close properly," said Liao Hanwen, deputy chief of the Tianjin Port's International Transport Inspection Operations Team. "And it will affect the environment."
If it is found that the imported goods are waste, the importing company is obliged to send the goods back to the country of origin. However, most of these companies do not want to cover the cost of return shipping.
"If we want to send these wastes to their original places, the countries that own these wastes will have to complete environmental procedures and documents," said Zhang Chheng, deputy director of the Customs and Excise Department of the Tianjin Customs Department. More related. "So this work is very complicated."
Since the beginning of this year, Tianjin Customs has successfully repatriated 14,600 tons of solid waste in cooperation with various departments and transport companies. But the port of Tianjin has more than 6,000 tons of solid waste left to be shipped back to its original source.
To solve this problem, Tianjin Customs has partnered with a local technology company to dispose of these wastes directly at the port, using the least environmentally friendly methods.
(China): China has planted more than 37 million hectares of cultivated land from 2011 to 2017, more than half of the policy for 2022, which aims to create about 67 million hectares of farmland by 2022. This is according to the Chinese Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs last week.
Lu Kuomin, an official from the ministry, said high-quality farmland was helping to significantly increase production yields across the country.
"The quality of the cultivated land is improving," said Lu Guomin, director of the Department of Farmland Construction at the Chinese Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs. The first benefit of this factor is increased productivity. For the 667-square-meter farm, production increased to more than 100 kilograms. The second benefit is water and energy savings. ”
More efficient use of cultivated land can save 24.3 percent water and 30.8 percent energy, Lu said. At the same time, the use of fertilizers and pesticides was reduced by 13.8 percent and 19.1 percent, respectively.
"Farmland like this is very good for agricultural industrialization and large-scale agricultural operations," he said. "The creation of high-quality cultivated land plays an important role in keeping China's crop production stable at more than 6 trillion kilograms."
