Understand the law: road traffic offenses
To answer the above question, FRESH NEWS 'Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation for this week, raises the topic of "Road Traffic Offenses" as follows:
In addition to being responsible for civil compensation to the victims or their families, the perpetrators of traffic accidents will face penalties under the provisions of the traffic offenses set out in the new 2015 Road Traffic Law. Traffic offenses can be misdemeanors with fines or imprisonment for not more than 6 (six) days, or can be misdemeanors depending on the nature of the negotiation offense and the damage caused by the offense. .
Road traffic offenses punishable by imprisonment and / or fines as stated in articles 69 to 90 of the Road Traffic Law 2015 if the following conditions are met:
* In the case of a person who obstructs a vehicle or refuses to stop a vehicle or refuses to submit to an alcohol or drug test, that person shall be punished with imprisonment as in the case of driving without a driver's license.
* In case of causing injury to another person, causing permanent disability, shall be punished by imprisonment from 1 to 3 years and / or a fine of 2,000,000 Riels to 6,000,000 Riels and shall be punished to the maximum Driving without a license, being intoxicated, causing injuries to many people and fleeing the scene in order to avoid responsibility.
Custodians who allow children to roam the streets causing accidents will be subject to civil liability, as well as for animal owners who cause their pets to walk all the way.
Please note that traffic offenses are criminal offenses, therefore, the payment of civil compensation to the victim or the victim's relatives is not a reason to dismiss the prosecution and the traffic offense case will be processed according to the criminal procedure in force. .
