(Seoul): A delegation from the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts led by Ms. Phoeung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts and President of the APSARA Authority, accompanied by the leadership of the APSARA Authority, attended a workshop on the results of the Preah Pitou Temple Conservation and Restoration Project on October 26, 2018 in Seoul, Republic of Korea.
Speaking at the opening ceremony, Mr. Jin OK-Sub, President of the Korea Cultural Heritage Foundation, said that what Korea is most proud of is contributing to the restoration of the reputation of Cambodia as a World Heritage Site and promoting Disseminate awareness of this work to the Korean people as well.
Ms. Phoeung Sakona said that the Korean Cultural Heritage Foundation, Korea International Development Agency (KOICA), the Korean Cultural Heritage Administration has provided technical and financial cooperation in the past to preserve and restore the Preah Pithu temple.
In particular, the cooperation between the Korean Cultural Heritage Foundation and the APSARA Authority has produced excellent achievements and has always received the support and appreciation of the ICC-Angkor Committee, so all these achievements are strong encouragement. To all relevant parties.
In order to share knowledge, the workshop also had two keynote presentations by the leadership of APSARA Authority on the topic of water management and tourism development at Angkor, in which international experts who participated in research at The Preah Pithu group of temples also presents important topics such as archeology, architecture, art history and conservation.
It should be reminded that the Korean Cultural Heritage Foundation started the project to restore and preserve the Preah Pithu temple group in 2015 and completed the first step by the end of 2018 and has trained various skills to Cambodian experts both inside and outside the past to share. Experience in conservation and management of World Heritage sites.
