Presidents and speakers: Fake news is a serious problem for the world, most of it comes from social media and websites with no clear source.
(Phnom Penh): Fake and fraudulent information is not new in the world, that is, it has existed and existed with people from the beginning, but as the world began to develop technology, fake information has evolved. Strong all over the world and Cambodia is also facing this problem.
Who created the fake news? Why do they create fake information? What are the effects of fake information? What should be done to be aware of fake information and prevent fake information?
The panelists and speakers at the workshop pointed out that as technology advances, fake and fraudulent sources of information often come from unrelated social media sites or online sources that distribute fake information online. More and more society is confusing the public, which is a major concern of the world.
According to Mr. Meas Sophorn, despite the technological advances and the many benefits, this fast-growing trend has also created challenges, especially those caused by fraudulent information. This has become a global problem that requires attention to be addressed effectively.
He said that in Cambodia, the issue of fraudulent information is becoming a major problem that requires the Royal Government, development partners, all stakeholders, together with the media, must work together to prevent the spread of fraudulent information that can cause Harm to national defense, national security, relations with other countries, national economy, public order, discrimination and national culture.
Undersecretary of State for the Ministry of Information stated that fraudulent information exists in all fields, including politics, economy, society, culture and more.
Leang De Lux, the new director general of e-news, said that the problem of fake and fraudulent information has been fought to prevent, both nationally, regionally and globally, but that the problem continues to spread. All must continue to participate in preventing this sharp problem to be more effective.
Speakers have observed that in order to gain one or more benefits for their business or for political gain, they have used methods to transmit or disseminate false information to the public through social media, websites or to make journalists Bound to the news writing, there is confusion and writing information without knowing it clearly.
Mr. Leang de Lux said that in order to participate in the prevention of false information, it starts with each of us, that is, when we know or see false information, we should spread it to those around us or our colleagues or relatives. To prevent the spread of false information.
Mr. Meas Sophorn continued that the Ministry of Information encourages all journalists on a number of points, including: adhering strictly to the professional ethics of journalists, strive to strengthen their professional capacity, increase activities to report to the place. Live to disseminate to the people to get real information, publish only real information, no fraud.
At the same time, he encouraged all journalists to work hard to produce articles that educate and educate the public about the consequences of false and misleading information.
Presidency and Workshop Speakers: False information is a serious problem for the world, mostly from social media and unsourced websites.
