Cambodia is committed to strengthening food security, food safety and quality to achieve the global vision of reducing hunger and malnutrition by 2030
(Switzerland): Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries Veng Sakhon has expressed Cambodia's commitment to strengthen food security, food safety and food quality to contribute to achieving a global vision to reduce hunger and malnutrition. Nutrition in 2030.
"Cambodia as well as other countries in the region are focusing on strengthening food security, food safety and quality to contribute to achieving a global vision to reduce hunger and malnutrition, as well as Reduce poverty and vulnerability of the people.
Speaking at the meeting, Ms. Gerda Verburg expressed many positive views on Cambodia in the development of the agricultural sector, especially on the development and investment in food security and nutrition, because Cambodia is a land conducive for agriculture.
She also emphasized the establishment of a food system between agriculture, fisheries and forestry in Cambodia, with the need to determine the geography of development, crop identification, monitoring of water management, climate change and biodiversity for assessment. The potential for development and investment.
"We must work together to support the partnership of the movement to increase nutrition interventions, starting with country-specific orientations to reduce hunger and malnutrition by 2030," she said.
In the near future, the United Nations will hold a training course on reducing hunger and malnutrition in Bangkok, and she hopes that the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries will appoint a focal point.
Taking this opportunity, the Minister thanked the United Nations for its good cooperation with Cambodia, and the Minister also welcomed the development projects and investment in agriculture in Cambodia.
He stressed that the Royal Government of Cambodia continues to pay attention to encouraging the private sector, especially investment in agriculture, and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries is willing to cooperate and support development projects and Invest in agriculture in Cambodia because Cambodia needs more resources to expand its potential in production, processing and export of agricultural products.
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries will conduct a training course on reducing hunger and malnutrition at the invitation of the United Nations, and the Minister requested that the United Nations continue to cooperate more closely in Promoting the movement to increase nutrition interventions in Cambodia.
It should be noted that this movement to increase nutrition interventions is a movement of the vision to ensure the complete reduction of malnutrition by 2030, which requires the participation of stakeholders such as government, civil society, the private sector, research institutions and other stakeholders. Participate in solving food security and nutrition issues.
Studies by some economists have shown that reducing malnutrition provides many benefits, such as increasing GDP, preventing annual child mortality from malnutrition, improving the quality of education, and increasing the number of children. Salary reduces poverty by age and increases the number of women in business.
The meeting emphasized that responsible agricultural investment refers to investment that is environmentally friendly, encourages support for farmers as producers, improves the livelihoods and livelihoods of farmers, children and women, and reduces the impact. To the environment. At the same time, the implementation of contract farming is also a mechanism responsible for marketing products and supporting the chain of sustainable production.
In addition, investment in agriculture must focus on waste management and yield loss to increase productivity, increase value added and reduce environmental impact.
The Meeting requested all stakeholders to continue to meet and discuss joint action plans between the state, the private sector, civil society, research institutions and development partners to achieve a common global vision to reduce hunger to zero. In the year 2030.
