Loved each other for 3 years, but his parents were not satisfied, suddenly saw the woman riding a motorcycle with her new boyfriend and jumped into the water to commit suicide
The shocking incident happened at 2:00 pm on October 23, 2018 at the point on the ballet bridge in Mittapheap village, Sangkat Russey Keo, Khan Russey Keo, Phnom Penh.
The victim was identified as Saing Surak, 21, a resident of Wat Sansom Kosal, Sangkat Boeung Tumpun I, Khan Meanchey. The girlfriend is 19-year-old Ear Chhayly, a first-year Vanda Accounting student living in Sangkat Boeung Tumpun II, Khan Meanchey, Phnom Penh.
The woman, who is her boyfriend, said that before the incident, she was riding a motorcycle with her new boyfriend around the Chroy Changvar development area in Chroy Changvar district. While riding a motorcycle taxi, suddenly appeared the man, his ex-girlfriend, who had been separated for 3 months.
After the incident, local authorities arrived at the scene and brought the woman to the Russey Keo police station for questioning to find out why. After the interrogation, the police contacted the two families to accept their daughter and search for the bodies of the victims who drowned.
A group of patients with anemia ask for the Ministry of Health to issue a national care and treatment policy for them. In Cambodia, there are about 3,000 such patients.
(Phnom Penh): A group of patients with hemophilia or blood clots want the Ministry of Health to issue national guidelines for care and treatment for this type of patient in other hospitals so that they can receive better medical services. Worry about health problems.
The president of the Blood Clotting Association claims that this group of patients is currently finding it difficult to find their own medical services, as there are only a handful of medical specialists in Cambodia who specialize in treating hemophilia in hospitals. National Children's Hospital and Angkor Hospital for Children.
Speaking at the 3rd General Assembly of the Cambodian Hemophilia Association this morning, October 23, Mr. Sem Sokpanha, President of the Cambodian Hemophilia Association, said that the Ministry of Health should expand the capacity of the team of specialists, including setting standards for treatment. Hemophilia to be treated technically without causing complications later.
He claimed that hemophilia or thrombocytopenia is a rare disease that is difficult to diagnose and treat, and if not treated in time, patients are at risk of disability and even death. Also.
According to Dr. Chean Sophal, a hemophilia specialist at the National Children's Hospital, the number of doctors who can treat hemophilia has now increased to nearly 10. In addition, the National Children's Hospital and Angkor Hospital for Children will continue to train new doctors to specialize in treating this type of disease as the number of patients increases.
However, the professor acknowledged that this type of treatment is expensive, costing more than $ 1,000 per treatment because the state can not yet afford it.
The doctor confirmed that in order to alleviate the burden of treatment of patients, the Cambodian Hemophilia Patients Association has helped to issue medicines for treatment because the Royal Government has exempted the import tax on this type of medicine.
Ms. Chhav Chhun Eng, who lives in Phnom Penh and has a son who has hemophilia, said that if there were enough doctors, she would not worry about this kind of disease that is happening to her child because it can be treated in time. Do not let the blood flow out of the body.
Hemophilia or thrombocytopenia is a rare disease among one in 10,000 people, and it is estimated that there are more than 3,000 cases in Cambodia.
According to the diagnosis, Cambodia has found 174 children with thrombosis and will have to live with treatment for life.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Hidehisa Horinouchi, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia, requested people to try to ride the public bus of Phnom Penh Capital Hall, especially the car that Japan provides assistance to Cambodia which is serving in Phnom Penh because these vehicles are Lots of specials.
In a short video, the ambassador confirmed that the new bus, a Japanese grant, had been in operation since October. He added that public buses are transporting more passengers every day than last year, and what is special is that the bus operation in Phnom Penh is based on management experience, as in Japan.
According to the same video, traveling by bus is very convenient because passengers can pay via the app available on the phone. For Japan's blue bus, Hidehisa Horinouchi confirmed that it is setting up a GPS system to automatically show the next stop and confirm the actual location through the App.
(Phnom Penh): A delegation of the Union of Cambodian Federation of Journalists (CPP) led by Mr. Huy Vannak will leave Cambodia on the night of October 23, 2018 for Moscow, Russia to attend the General Assembly. The 10th International Conference of Asian Political Parties (ICAPP) from October 24-27, 2018.
At this year's ICAPP General Assembly in Russia, the press will cover two topics. The first topic is: the role of political parties in reducing international tensions and in reducing extremism. The second topic is: the importance of inter-political cooperation in the formation and development of political, economic, social and state-building relations. Mr. Huy Vannak will address the ICAPP General Assembly on the second topic.
