Discover Switzerland where Samdech Techo Hun Sen Attends UN Conference on Trade and Development of the 2018 World Investment Forum
Switzerland has been ranked as one of the happiest and safest countries in the world, making it a popular tourist destination, a gathering place for banks, the headquarters of major international organizations, and a venue for meetings. International as well. Here is some important information about Switzerland:
* Topography and geographical location:
Switzerland is located in western and central Europe, bordering Italy to the south, France to the west, Germany to the north and Austria and Liechtenstein to the east, without sea borders. Because much of the territory is covered by the Alps, most of the population is concentrated in the highlands, which are located in the center of the country and in large clusters of cities.
The Swiss Federal Constitution, which came into force in 1848, is the legal basis of the modern federal state. The country's constitution has become the oldest in the world, being revised in 1999, but not much has changed.
Since 1959, the Swiss government has had four major parties, each with a majority in the lower house of parliament and a member of the federal parliament. The Supreme Court, on the other hand, is responsible for receiving major complaints, which are elected by the federal parliament for a six-year term.
With no sea borders, Switzerland has no navy. However, as there is a large lake next to the neighboring country, the Swiss army used ships to patrol there. Swiss citizens are barred from serving in the military abroad, except for Vatican patrolmen, or if they are dual nationals.
The structure of the military system in Switzerland states that they can keep weapons in their own homes. The arms control policy is the same in Europe as a whole, with about 29 percent of the population legally owning weapons, most of which are owned by conscripts. Some organizations and political parties have expressed opposition to the issue, but most of the public still agrees.
After the end of the Cold War, some activists wanted to prevent or eliminate the military. A significant referendum was held on November 26, 1989 by the military opposition. Unfortunately, the referendum failed because a two-thirds majority voted against a proposal to eliminate the military.
Switzerland is a country of stability, prosperity, high economic growth and prosperity until it is considered to have the highest GDP per capita. Zurich has become home to 1.5 million people and 150 companies, the world's most important economic center. Switzerland is also ranked as one of the world's most powerful economies.
Industry has become an important part of driving the Swiss economy. Major industrial products include pharmaceuticals, healthcare products, pharmaceuticals, institute products, precision measuring instruments, clocks and musical instruments.
In terms of services, Switzerland has a reputation for banking, insurance, tourism and providing a secure location for international organizations. Of all the developed countries, this Western European country also ranks as one of the lowest tax collectors. It ranks 20th out of 189 countries in the world in terms of ease of doing business.
The education sector in Switzerland is very diverse because the country's constitution gives the provinces and municipalities the authority to organize their own education system. Unlike other countries in Switzerland, there are both public and private schools as well as international private schools. The minimum age for entering primary school can range from 4 to 6 years, depending on the area, but most schools offer free tuition for children entering the age group of 4 or 5.
There are 12 universities in Switzerland, and the oldest and earliest university was discovered in 1460, located in the city of Basel, which specializes in medicine, which has always maintained a research background in chemistry and medicine. The largest university in the country is Zurich University, which has about 25,000 students.
Switzerland has become a haven for important individuals who have contributed to the creation of literature, art, music and science to this day. Not only that, Switzerland also attracted a lot of creative people to live here during the war and training in Europe.
Traditional farming and herding culture continue to be maintained in many parts of the country, while small farms are still present far from the city. Folk art is still alive and well throughout the country, expressed through music, dance, poetry, wood carving and weaving.
