The Minister of Cult hopes that the Khmer Acharya will do good deeds for the society, either by educating or disciplining his children according to the Buddhist Dharma
(Phnom Penh): Speaking at the opening ceremony of the training course on the role, duties and ethics of the 30th generation of Khmer priests on October 19, 2018, with the participation of 155 trainees, Senior Minister Him Chhem, Minister of Cult and Religion It is clear that our Khmer Acharya will do great deeds for the benefit of society, either by educating or disciplining his children according to the Buddhist Dharma.
Mr. Hem Chhem stated that the achievements that will come out of this training are not only the pride and benefit for the trainees, but also the pride of the whole society, we will get more resources. Others have the correct knowledge of ancient rules to contribute to and contribute to the process of social development by preserving our beautiful culture and national identity.
"Through this training, I, Your Majesty, hope that our trainees will do things that are beneficial to society, including educating or educating their children to cultivate emotional awareness," he said. "The high moral values that are inherited from our ancestors and the practice of our Buddhist path."
Senior Minister Him Chhem said that Cambodian ancestors have left many valuable cultural and cultural heritages that make the world admire and recognize Cambodian works. Therefore, the practice of various rituals is contrary to the ancient rules and is a violation of the values and identity of the nation itself.
The minister said that Cambodia is a country with a rich culture and traditions and a very old people in the region. Therefore, every Buddhist ceremony, tradition or archeology needs to have a scholar called an acharya to guide the public to practice correctly and unanimously.
According to Senior Minister Him Chhem, as the leader of various ceremonies, it is necessary to follow the ancient rules left by the ancestors, and the implementation must be consistent from one area to another to avoid Contradictory practices lead to conflicts in celebrations caused by violations of ancient rules and subsequent duplication of practices.
