(Thailand): China and Thailand have signed a memorandum of understanding to develop cooperation on various water resources projects under the special fund of the Lancang Mekong Cooperation. The signing ceremony took place in Bangkok, Thailand last Thursday.
Chinese Ambassador to Thailand Li Yuqian and Permanent Secretary of the Thai Ministry of Water Resources and Environment Vijam Shimachaya signed the MoU. Representatives from the member countries of the Lancang Mekong Cooperation also attended the event.
In order to further promote the concept of "building a community with a united future for humanity", the Lancang Mekong Cooperation is a sub-regional cooperation mechanism that connects the six countries along the river, namely: Cambodia, China, Laos, Myanmar, Thailand and Vietnam. The vision of this mechanism is to contribute to the economic and social development of the subregion.
After the establishment of this mechanism, many fruitful results have been achieved on water resources management. Through the Mekong River Commission, China has been continuously providing information about the river to the lower reaches of the river during the high tide for 16 years.
"After the signing of the MoU, we will start implementing this program, especially in areas with sensitive ecosystems," said Vijam Simachaya, Permanent Secretary of the Thai Ministry of Water Resources and Environment. "We will work together to improve water quality and climate change resilience."
(China): A government-sponsored program in China has lifted people with disabilities out of poverty and improved their lives. On the occasion of China's National Poverty Alleviation Day, which took place on Tuesday this week, the Chinese government's Information Bureau held a special event in Beijing and invited some disabled people to share their heartbreaking stories. Of them.
Among the disabled was a farmer, Li Chien, whose life deteriorated after he lost one of his arms while working as a firefighter. After a while, his onion business lost almost everything. As the situation worsened, Li also heard that the Chinese government could help his farming business.
For the past two years, he has been learning about farming skills and the basics of marketing. In 2016, he was able to earn a total of 130,000 yuan from growing onions and other crops, as well as receiving dividends from a program offered to low-income people.
Financial assistance and training, which is the principle of the Chinese government, also helped Xie Liu. As a child with a disability, he says he has been afraid of others because he does not feel like he can do anything like his friends. But with the help of many authorities, Sear not only learned the art of massage for the disabled, but also regained his self-confidence.
Chea Ru is the director of an industrial park that specializes in helping people with disabilities create jobs, businesses and how to get a job. He described the struggles of many disabled employees and hoped that more and more people could begin to appreciate their struggles.
"This little mask and all the other small products that people with disabilities can make contain all their hopes and dreams," she said. They use real love and effort to produce it. They hope that these products can meet the needs of customers and can share their love with everyone who uses their products.
Most of the millions of people with disabilities in China continue to receive government assistance. It is hoped that more such programs will be available to benefit more people in need and to provide better opportunities for them. (China CCTV) Three trains ordered from Mexico Arrival at the airport will arrive in Cambodia at the end of next month
The three trains will be used to transport passengers from the station to Phnom Penh International Airport. All three trains are more modern than the current trains, running at a high speed of only 25 minutes from the station to the airport.
The train has two locomotives, one front and one rear, with the latest technology, high safety, flexible airplane-like seats, a bathroom, air conditioning, automatic doors and side luggage compartments. Passengers will have Wifi, TV and video information system.
Please be informed that in order to facilitate the travel of passengers to Phnom Penh International Airport, on Tuesday, April 10, 2018, the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Public Works, Royal Railway Cambodia, launched a light rail train carrying passengers from the station. In Srah Chak district, enter the international airport.
The launch of this line has received a lot of support from passengers. But there have also been many problems in the past, such as traffic accidents, noise and dust for people traveling along the railway built on the road.
Three new trains ordered from Mexico are expected to help solve all these problems, as the new trains are more modern, lighter and have better braking than current trains.
