The Minister of Commerce said this on the occasion of presiding over the inauguration of the Khmer Product Trade Fair of the Khmer Product Purchasing Campaign (Season 3), which was held on October 18, 2018. The purpose of this exhibition is to awaken the Cambodian people to support and use locally produced goods instead of imported goods.
The Minister highlighted some of the opportunities and challenges of Cambodia after joining the ASEAN Economic Community (AEC), especially the implementation of a free trade agreement on the flow of goods and services that allows all member countries to have free movement of goods and services. Set in the ASEAN region.
In response, Mr. Pan Sosak called for the participation of all stakeholders, especially producers, entrepreneurs and the Cambodian people.
Mr. Pan Sosak said, "While the Ministry of Commerce is trying to find new markets for the private sector, all producers and entrepreneurs need to know how to develop and improve the quality of products with high innovation to compete with imported goods and people. "People need to support national products by using and stimulating more buying movements to support local production and enhance Cambodia's competitiveness."
The Minister also mentioned the intervention of the Ministry of Commerce to reduce the presence of foreign products in the market by compiling and implementing trade liberalization laws and facilitating the Cambodian private sector to participate in international exhibition events to promote and make Diversify (expand) the export of Cambodian products to foreign markets.
It should be noted that the Cambodian Product Trade Fair event is part of the market development policy initiated by the Ministry of Commerce, which is in line with the third pillar goal on private sector development and the work of the fourth phase of the Royal Government's Rectangular Strategy. Cambodia.
So far, the Ministry of Commerce has successfully organized this campaign for two seasons: the first season in Siem Reap, Kampong Cham, Sihanoukville and Phnom Penh, and the second season in Stung Treng, Battambang, Svay Rieng, Phnom Penh and Kep. Separately, for the third season, the Ministry of Commerce has identified six capitals-provinces, including: Kandal, Kampong Chhnang, Banteay Meanchey, Prey Veng, Koh Kong, and Phnom Penh as the venue for trade fairs.
