Kampong Cham Provincial Commissioner Inspects Construction of 2 Administrative Police Stations in Srey Santhor District
(Kampong Cham): This morning, October 18, 2018, after completing the mission in Kang Meas district, Brigadier General Chea Cheang, Deputy Commissioner for Planning, Logistics and Finance, Representative of Major General Em Kosal, Provincial Police Commissioner of Kampong Cham and Police Inspector Srey Santhor district inspected the construction of 2 administrative police stations.
The two administrative police stations, including the Prek Po administrative police station in Tuol Ry Leu village, Prek Po commune, Srey Santhor district, are 9.4 meters long and 7.2 meters wide. .
Separately, Meanchey Administrative Police Station is located in Veal Village, Meanchey Commune, Srey Santhor District, which is a project of the Ministry of Interior, with the Construction Department of the General Department of Logistics and Finance, Ministry of Interior, which is 100% completed.
For the remaining work, Brigadier General Cheang instructed all the craftsmen to pay more attention to the work with high responsibility to avoid gaps eventually.
Senior Minister Meas Sophea: CMAC forces will not be disbanded even after the end of its mission after 2025
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Meas Sophea, Senior Minister in charge of Mine Action, UXO and Engineering of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, has confirmed that the CMAC force will not be disbanded even after completing its mission after 2025. CMAC may plan to establish a national demining institute in the future to transfer that knowledge to future generations.
Senior Minister Meas Sophea's statement was made on October 18, 2018, during a meeting with Mr. Heng Ratana, Director General of the Cambodian Mine Action Center (CMAC) at CMAC Headquarters. At the same time, CMAC officials are highly committed to clearing the area of landmines and unexploded ordnance by 2025.
Mr. Heng Ratana added that for the post-2025 period, CMAC is planning to increase the number of professional officers who can become experts for further training in other countries and locally.
Mr. Heng Ratana stated that since its operation more than 20 years ago, CMAC has destroyed 2.7 million landmines and unexploded ordnance, about 70 percent of the remaining mines and 53 percent of the mines. Total number of landmines. For the past nine months, CMAC has cleared more than 60 square kilometers in a planned 120 square kilometers and found more than 30,000 landmines and unexploded ordnance.
On this occasion, Senior Minister Meas Sophea also expressed his appreciation to CMAC for achieving a difficult mission to serve the needs of the Cambodian people. The senior minister also urged CMAC to continue this mission, while demining must be developed in rural areas, along with schools, roads, water wells for the people.
