While US Secretary of Defense is in Vietnam, B-52 bombers are ordered to fly over the South China Sea!
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States on Tuesday sent two B-52 bombers to fly over disputed islands in the South China Sea. This is according to US forces in the Pacific quoted by CNN in the morning of Thursday, October 18, 2018.
"Two B-52H bombers from Guam have been conducting regular operations in the South China Sea, and this is part of a series of operations," the US Pacific Command told CNN. "The operation continues the presence of US Indo-Pacific Bomber bombers, which began in March 2004."
This is not the first time that US bombers have flown in the disputed South China Sea, which China claims as its own and has always been. US military action in the conflict zone is considered provocative and tense in the region.
