(Phnom Penh): Investment in the construction sector in Cambodia continues to increase compared to the first nine months of 2018 to 2017. This is according to the report of the Ministry of Land Management and Urban Planning.
The report on the performance of the Ministry of Land Management and Urban Planning for the first nine months of 2018, released on October 17, 2018, shows that in the first nine months of 2018, there were 2,541 construction projects on the land. Nearly 11 million square meters, with an estimated investment of nearly 5 billion US dollars.
Of these projects, 224 were managed by the Ministry of Land Management and Construction and 2,317 were managed by the Capital-Provincial Hall.
The report also points out that of these construction projects, 2,151 are residential projects with an investment capital of nearly 2 billion US dollars. As of 2000 to the first nine months of 2018, there were 42,810 housing projects nationwide with an investment capital of over 40,000 million US dollars.
According to reports, through the construction sector, this has also contributed to providing employment to many Cambodians. In the first nine months of 2018, the construction sector employed an average of 200,000 to 220,000 people per day. In Phnom Penh, on average, there are between 90,000 and 100,000 construction workers per day. Compared to the same period in 2017, employment in the construction sector provided employment to Cambodians increased by 120 percent.
According to the report, unskilled workers can earn between 35,000 and 40,000 riel per day, while skilled workers and masters can earn 50,000 to 75,000 riel per day. Engineers, architects and other professionals can earn between $ 400 and $ 2,500. Japan and Cambodia will hold 18 special events a month to celebrate the 65th anniversary of their relationship
