(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Chhay Thorn, Minister of Planning, on October 17, 2018, opened a workshop on Producers, Data Users and Signing Ceremony for the Implementation Agreement between the 2019 Agricultural Census and the Agricultural Survey. Years.
Traditionally, the agricultural census is conducted every 10 years, but due to 2018, the Ministry did not have enough funds, so it was postponed to 2019.
"Therefore, 2018 is the year that the National Institute of Statistics conducts a semi-agricultural census to update agricultural and fisheries data," said Senior Minister Chhay Thorn. That is why we are meeting today to discuss this important plan. It should also be reminded that because in 2018 we did not receive enough funds to operate this project, we postponed this work until 2019.
The Minister said that the Royal Government of Cambodia, with the Ministry of Planning and the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries as its staff, is committed to conducting the mid-2019 agricultural census and the upcoming annual data collection. Successfully and without forgetting to work with development partners such as the FAO and the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA).
He said that on the other hand, the implementation of the project between the 2019 agricultural census will use existing mechanisms such as the 2013 Agricultural Census Technical Committee, which is composed of the Ministry of Planning, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, as well as other relevant government institutions.
According to Senior Minister Chhay Thorn, according to the master plan, the mid-2019 agricultural census is divided into four major phases:
* Phase 1: Capacity building phase, focusing on capacity building of core officials at the national and sub-national levels of the Ministry of Planning and the Ministry of Agriculture through the establishment of training courses on collection, analysis, dissemination and In particular, training on the use of tablets for information retrieval, which is the latest technology that is popular in the work of statistics in this modern age.
Phase 2: The actual data collection phase at the sampled basis, with plans to use tablets instead of paper, which costs a lot of money.
Phase 3: A phase that focuses on in-depth data analysis, including the production of agricultural and fisheries statistics maps to the smallest level.
* Phase 4: A phase focused on expanding the scope of dissemination of agricultural and fisheries data by all means, including the publication of such data into the Website, which can lead to Another level of convenience for users of statistical data at all levels, from national to sub-national and from regional to international.
